#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048423 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048157 :topic/math-academy-id 394 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations Using a Difference of Squares"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832 :topic/math-academy-id 1422 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations with Leading Coefficients by Factoring"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050497 :topic/math-academy-id 393 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations with No Constant Term"}] :topic/id #uuid "9ddcc703-a984-5097-a142-236dc3c2fbaf" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157170 :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/math-academy-id "e-7836" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1}  = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x-1}-2x{e}^{x-1}-15{e}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-15){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15  = 0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157171 :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7838" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1}-16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-16){3}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3},$ and their product is $\\left( -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2979 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050617 :topic/math-academy-id 2859 :topic/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Exponential Functions and Polynomials"}] :topic/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814 :topic/math-academy-id 1849 :topic/title "Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178 :topic/math-academy-id 1374 :topic/title "Solving 3x3 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}] :topic/id #uuid "a4bbeec2-7e3f-58f1-87d1-563d58d73752" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119616 :question/id #uuid "fd3cb5b1-f16e-5a52-82e4-25bbbd1c4fe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4789" :question/problem "Find $s,$ if $3\\mathbf{a_1}+s\\mathbf{a_2} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n![](images/c7/c715fd50dbcc9251cd7c01e033ba59be2af244d57ec02b707aa7cd8764e58d58.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $s$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+s{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $s=4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for One Coefficient in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119617 :question/id #uuid "548ef945-392b-5e75-9b60-14f4e2de6261" :question/math-academy-id "e-4790" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{x},$ if $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n![](images/8f/8fef8bb1e88ca823b49d8c5544c5456c66b7a5beb0c9bd48f349a50bf3fa17b5.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2x & =b \\\\ 2x & =b-a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(b-a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Vector in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119618 :question/id #uuid "13ebde04-8518-5837-9420-c9487debc562" :question/math-academy-id "e-4791" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n![](images/0b/0b7919416954fe536a93f681fb3c56cac8dddff67e389ef233fdb242390dfb91.svg)\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2  = \\mathbf d,$ then what is $x_1+x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "If $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2  = \\mathbf d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $x_1=5,$ which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ 4(5)+{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(5)+(-15) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15,$ which means\n\n$$\n5 \\mathbf{c_1}-15 \\mathbf{c_2} = \\mathbf{d}.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2=5-15=-10.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119669 :question/id #uuid "d9beff9d-db58-540f-811e-a07fb77d3d92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6191" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n![](images/16/1687c0a08fedfd90a26481007bb9bf56263edf67574fd690662064ef0341c86c.svg)\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf b,$ then what is $x_1+x_2+x_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a_1}, \\,\\mathbf{a_2}$ and $\\mathbf{a_3}$ with coefficients $x_1,\\, x_2$ and $x_3,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n![](images/9b/9bf913744317fab321cf9f2e01da732853a52a4fd7948c65da596ae4706f6dce.svg)\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 = 2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_3=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+2(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_1 = -3.\n$$\n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=-3, x_2=-1, x_3=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3 \\mathbf{a_1}- \\mathbf{a_2} +2 \\mathbf{a_3}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=-3-1+2=-2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Three Coefficients in a Linear Combination"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1851 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315 :topic/math-academy-id 1852 :topic/title "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045 :topic/math-academy-id 2094 :topic/title "The Dot Product in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/math-academy-id 1853 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space"}] :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/math-academy-id 1866 :topic/title "The Dimension of a Span"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009 :topic/math-academy-id 2623 :topic/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :topic/id #uuid "26e7d665-1fe7-5eaf-8ec8-486cb95ff36f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119661 :question/id #uuid "7cce27e9-e2b2-599c-9f4e-437caf1a23ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-5415" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Rank of a Matrix Given the Number of Pivot Columns"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119662 :question/id #uuid "a6a3db41-6033-537a-bc9d-03fde02dc869" :question/math-academy-id "e-5417" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n![](images/2c/2cd43e61d4c51ee86d78217688daea5f8ebdfa656a9628e4bef3ef4ac9f8bd3a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119663 :question/id #uuid "b3e4eb01-0669-586b-a716-aef87d1c3580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5418" :question/problem "If ![](images/82/823965b7b8f818ec843aefc4c4cc16ef2922e269db8d53f37cf633ff132e5783.svg), find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1867 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539 :topic/math-academy-id 1963 :topic/title "The Image and Rank of a Linear Transformation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996 :topic/math-academy-id 1868 :topic/title "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix"}] :topic/title "The Rank of a Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/math-academy-id 3344 :topic/title "Visualizing Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/math-academy-id 1939 :topic/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector"}] :topic/id #uuid "6e3d5713-6e26-51db-8b8f-61d36b28e342" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179574 :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/f9/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3d/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/99/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179576 :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179581 :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9de4a1c2dfb6b8939b2440930eefb8b7020947bd7b943021214adf4cbfce735.png)\n\n    \n\nWhich of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/b8/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3692 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049147 :topic/math-academy-id 3694 :topic/title "Interpreting Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049765 :topic/math-academy-id 3701 :topic/title "Properties of the Divergence Operator"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049826 :topic/math-academy-id 3700 :topic/title "Properties of the Curl Operator"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"}] :topic/title "Gradient Vector Fields"}]}
